{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"3685c155-aafc-4017-9de3-5f11a00f6751","title":"Uniform circular motion and centripetal force"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3685c155-aafc-4017-9de3-5f11a00f6751","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Uniform circular motion means constant speed around a circle. The velocity points along the tangent, so its direction keeps changing.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"C1","content":"Uniform circular motion"},{"op":"figure","id":"orbit","region":"left","frame":{"x":[-3.5,3.5],"y":[-2.7,3.5],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"path","center":[0,0],"r":2},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0],"label":"O"},{"kind":"point","id":"top","at":[0,2]},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"top"},"stroke":"dashed","label":"r"},{"kind":"segment","id":"velocity","from":{"at":"top"},"to":[-1.7,2],"arrow":true,"label":"v"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"speed","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"v=\\frac{2\\pi r}{T}\\quad\\text{(constant speed)}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3685c155-aafc-4017-9de3-5f11a00f6751","step":{"id":"s2","script":"A quarter-turn later, the velocity points upward. Changing velocity requires acceleration, even though the speed stays constant.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"turn","on":"orbit","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"rightpoint","at":[2,0]},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"rightpoint"},"to":[2,1.7],"arrow":true,"label":"v"},{"kind":"arc","center":{"at":"center"},"r":2.3,"start":5,"end":80,"arrow":true}]},{"op":"text","id":"change","anchor":{"el":"speed","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"Constant speed ≠ constant velocity"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If speed is constant, why is there still acceleration?","expected_gist":"Velocity changes direction."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3685c155-aafc-4017-9de3-5f11a00f6751","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The acceleration points toward the center. Its magnitude increases with speed squared and decreases as the radius grows.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"accel","on":"orbit","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"rightpoint"},"to":{"at":"center"},"arrow":true,"label":"a꜀"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"accelform","anchor":{"el":"change","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"a_c=\\frac{v^2}{r}\\quad\\text{(toward center)}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3685c155-aafc-4017-9de3-5f11a00f6751","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Newton’s second law gives the required inward net force. Centripetal describes its role, not an extra force: tension, gravity, or friction can provide it.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"force","on":"orbit","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"top"},"to":[0,0.55],"arrow":true,"label":"F net"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"forceform","anchor":{"el":"accelform","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"F_{\\text{net,inward}}=ma_c=\\frac{mv^2}{r}"},{"op":"text","id":"source","anchor":{"el":"forceform","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"Double the speed → four times the force"}],"checkpoint":null}}
